      "id": 4,
      "kind": "mother_usage",
      "payload": "{\"model_calls\":1,\"phase\":\"preparation\",\"plan\":{\"A\":\"Prioritize the likely bottleneck: exact-output serialization. Produce a serial candidate that preserves the current sort and permutation traversal but replaces per-character iostream insertion and per-permutation `std::endl` flushing with a bounded buffered byte writer. Retain the precise `[ `, character-plus-space, `]\\\\n` layout, the six-character whitespace-removal rule, and empty-input behavior; never retain the entire factorial-sized output in memory.\",\"B\":\"Independently investigate a low-overhead serial permutation/formatting path. Compare the existing generic traversal with a specialized in-place string/line representation or an equivalent standard permutation primitive, while preserving the baseline’s character ordering, duplicate handling, lexicographic order, and byte-for-byte output. Focus on eliminating repeated formatting/callback work without relying on input-specific counts or precomputed answers; provide a separately measurable candidate rather than assuming it combines beneficially with A.\",\"C\":\"Assess whether four ARM64 cores can help despite the inherently ordered stdout stream. Explore only bounded-memory ordered decomposition of lexicographic permutation ranges/prefixes, with a single ordered emitter and exact handling of repeated characters. Output serialization is likely dominant, so reject parallel designs whose synchronization, buffering, or ordered merge loses to the best serial candidate; do not use unbounded whole-output buffering or alter the fixed build/target setup.\",\"G\":\"Integrate only evidence-backed candidates from A, B, and C under the unchanged harness. First verify byte-exact behavior across empty/whitespace-only input, one character, duplicates, and varied character ordering, then compare each candidate and only selected combinations against the valid 0.133006465 s baseline. Choose the fastest correct coherent program; do not assume gains are additive, and retain the existing semantics for whitespace stripping, ordering, and output formatting.\",\"analysis\":\"The only measurement is a valid 4-case ARM64 baseline at 0.133006465 s; there is no profile or prior optimized result. This program emits every distinct permutation, so output work grows factorially. The source performs many stream insertions per line and calls `std::endl` for every permutation, making buffered exact serialization the strongest initial hypothesis. Parallel generation is uncertain because lexicographic output must remain globally ordered and stdout is serial. All candidates must remain correct for every valid input and preserve the source’s observable behavior exactly.\"},\"request_sha256\":\"ea07ba0170a4a210f06775be1e50a12e92dd0b8c29ed0488f4ce97c16505911a\",\"response\":{\"data\":{\"A\":\"Prioritize the likely bottleneck: exact-output serialization. Produce a serial candidate that preserves the current sort and permutation traversal but replaces per-character iostream insertion and per-permutation `std::endl` flushing with a bounded buffered byte writer. Retain the precise `[ `, character-plus-space, `]\\\\n` layout, the six-character whitespace-removal rule, and empty-input behavior; never retain the entire factorial-sized output in memory.\",\"B\":\"Independently investigate a low-overhead serial permutation/formatting path. Compare the existing generic traversal with a specialized in-place string/line representation or an equivalent standard permutation primitive, while preserving the baseline’s character ordering, duplicate handling, lexicographic order, and byte-for-byte output. Focus on eliminating repeated formatting/callback work without relying on input-specific counts or precomputed answers; provide a separately measurable candidate rather than assuming it combines beneficially with A.\",\"C\":\"Assess whether four ARM64 cores can help despite the inherently ordered stdout stream. Explore only bounded-memory ordered decomposition of lexicographic permutation ranges/prefixes, with a single ordered emitter and exact handling of repeated characters. Output serialization is likely dominant, so reject parallel designs whose synchronization, buffering, or ordered merge loses to the best serial candidate; do not use unbounded whole-output buffering or alter the fixed build/target setup.\",\"G\":\"Integrate only evidence-backed candidates from A, B, and C under the unchanged harness. First verify byte-exact behavior across empty/whitespace-only input, one character, duplicates, and varied character ordering, then compare each candidate and only selected combinations against the valid 0.133006465 s baseline. Choose the fastest correct coherent program; do not assume gains are additive, and retain the existing semantics for whitespace stripping, ordering, and output formatting.\",\"analysis\":\"The only measurement is a valid 4-case ARM64 baseline at 0.133006465 s; there is no profile or prior optimized result. This program emits every distinct permutation, so output work grows factorially. The source performs many stream insertions per line and calls `std::endl` for every permutation, making buffered exact serialization the strongest initial hypothesis. Parallel generation is uncertain because lexicographic output must remain globally ordered and stdout is serial. All candidates must remain correct for every valid input and preserve the source’s observable behavior exactly.\"},\"error\":null,\"infrastructure_error\":false,\"model\":\"gpt-5.6-terra\",\"nonce\":\"4bf818e5db9a4ae3bec75bf2104b19e9\",\"protocol\":\"hpc-forest-codex-mailbox-v1\",\"reasoning_effort\":\"max\",\"request_sha256\":\"ea07ba0170a4a210f06775be1e50a12e92dd0b8c29ed0488f4ce97c16505911a\",\"session_id\":\"15219a6fd62b4dcb91b395dcd4ae03d5\",\"usage\":{\"cached_tokens_included_in_input\":true,\"codex_jsonl_sha256\":\"f8e5070bc9072c4d8d4c326e35d312b350f9a3a9cefb54fe7cff61c7d9e703d0\",\"events\":[{\"cache_write_input_token